1
00:00:04,138 --> 00:00:06,673
♪ In the
not-too-distant future ♪

2
00:00:06,673 --> 00:00:10,010
♪ Next Sunday A.D. ♪

3
00:00:10,010 --> 00:00:12,813
♪ There was a guy named Joel ♪

4
00:00:12,813 --> 00:00:16,050
♪ Not too different
from you or me ♪

5
00:00:16,050 --> 00:00:18,920
♪ He worked at
Gizmonic Institute ♪

6
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:22,357
♪ Just another face
in a red jumpsuit ♪

7
00:00:22,357 --> 00:00:25,060
♪ He did a good job
cleaning up the place ♪

8
00:00:25,060 --> 00:00:26,593
♪ But his bosses
didn't like him ♪

9
00:00:26,593 --> 00:00:31,498
♪ So they shot him into space ♪

10
00:00:31,498 --> 00:00:34,735
♪ We'll send him cheesy movies ♪

11
00:00:34,735 --> 00:00:37,005
♪ The worst we can find ♪

12
00:00:37,005 --> 00:00:38,305
♪ La la la ♪

13
00:00:38,305 --> 00:00:40,775
♪ He'll have to sit
and watch them all ♪

14
00:00:40,775 --> 00:00:43,077
♪ And we'll monitor his mind ♪

15
00:00:43,077 --> 00:00:44,378
♪ La la la ♪

16
00:00:44,378 --> 00:00:47,015
♪ Now, keep in mind
Joel can't control ♪

17
00:00:47,015 --> 00:00:49,250
♪ Where the movies
begin or end ♪

18
00:00:49,250 --> 00:00:50,485
♪ La la la ♪

19
00:00:50,485 --> 00:00:53,253
♪ Because he used
those special parts ♪

20
00:00:53,253 --> 00:00:58,158
♪ To make his robot friends ♪

21
00:00:58,158 --> 00:01:00,295
♪ Robot roll call ♪

22
00:01:00,295 --> 00:01:01,362
♪ Cambot ♪
♪ Pan left ♪

23
00:01:01,362 --> 00:01:02,730
♪ Gypsy ♪
♪ Hi, girl ♪

24
00:01:02,730 --> 00:01:04,532
♪ Tom Servo ♪

25
00:01:04,532 --> 00:01:06,000
♪ Crow ♪

26
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,570
♪ If you're wondering how
he eats and breathes ♪

27
00:01:08,570 --> 00:01:11,038
♪ And other science facts ♪

28
00:01:11,038 --> 00:01:12,507
♪ La la la ♪

29
00:01:12,507 --> 00:01:13,475
♪ Then repeat to yourself,
"It's just a show, ♪

30
00:01:13,475 --> 00:01:14,842
♪ "I should really just relax" ♪

31
00:01:14,842 --> 00:01:19,047
♪ For Mystery Science
Theater 3000 ♪

32
00:01:32,493 --> 00:01:36,063
- IBM, CBS, RCA, the
CIA, the FBI, the NBA,

33
00:01:37,298 --> 00:01:41,535
A&P, NBC, ABC, R. Crumb,
and C.P.O. Sharkey.

34
00:01:41,535 --> 00:01:43,270
You see, they're all the same.

35
00:01:43,270 --> 00:01:44,838
And what do I think?

36
00:01:44,838 --> 00:01:48,810
I think, if life gives you
lemons, you make lemonade.

37
00:01:48,810 --> 00:01:50,912
Good night, and may God bless.

38
00:01:50,912 --> 00:01:53,482
- Hey, that was great.

39
00:01:53,482 --> 00:01:54,748
- Hey, I didn't know
you guys were there.

40
00:01:54,748 --> 00:01:56,617
- Stop, thank you.

41
00:01:56,617 --> 00:01:58,152
Well, it's a work in progress.

42
00:01:58,152 --> 00:02:02,323
It's part of my one-man show
called "Robot on the Run."

43
00:02:02,323 --> 00:02:03,658
- Hey, look, we're
on, everybody.

44
00:02:03,658 --> 00:02:04,892
Hi!

45
00:02:04,892 --> 00:02:06,227
Hey, welcome to the
Satellite of Love.

46
00:02:06,227 --> 00:02:08,997
We're having our art
Chautauqua today.

47
00:02:08,997 --> 00:02:11,498
- [Woman] Commercial
sign in 15 seconds.

48
00:02:11,498 --> 00:02:13,400
- Anyway, Tom, you
wanna do yours?

49
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:14,335
- Let's do it.

50
00:02:14,335 --> 00:02:15,603
(Tom clears throat)

51
00:02:15,603 --> 00:02:18,940
I was born the son of a
poor Filipino merchant.

52
00:02:18,940 --> 00:02:20,675
I remember I would
sit on the stoop

53
00:02:20,675 --> 00:02:23,978
of my tenement brownstone
on the Lower East Side.

54
00:02:23,978 --> 00:02:24,812
I was...

55
00:02:26,915 --> 00:02:29,150
- [Woman] Tom, your
line is, "I was crying."

56
00:02:29,150 --> 00:02:30,685
- Thank you.

57
00:02:30,685 --> 00:02:32,887
- [Woman] Commercial sign
in five, four, three, two,

58
00:02:32,887 --> 00:02:33,955
commercial sign now.

59
00:02:33,955 --> 00:02:35,723
- I was crying.

60
00:02:35,723 --> 00:02:37,158
(Tom crying)

61
00:02:37,158 --> 00:02:39,727
(upbeat music)

62
00:02:43,798 --> 00:02:45,567
(Tom crying)

63
00:02:45,567 --> 00:02:46,767
- Tom?

64
00:02:46,767 --> 00:02:49,270
Mayo, I'm going to
make a recommendation

65
00:02:49,270 --> 00:02:51,505
that you be dropped
from the program.

66
00:02:51,505 --> 00:02:52,807
- Don't you do it!

67
00:02:52,807 --> 00:02:54,242
Don't you do it!

68
00:02:54,242 --> 00:02:56,243
I've got nowhere else to go!

69
00:02:56,243 --> 00:02:57,778
I've got nothin'.

70
00:02:57,778 --> 00:02:58,713
- Hey, pretty good.

71
00:02:58,713 --> 00:03:00,248
- Yeah, pretty good.

72
00:03:00,248 --> 00:03:03,283
- Okay, anyway,
I think it's time

73
00:03:03,283 --> 00:03:04,952
to do the invention
exchange here.

74
00:03:04,952 --> 00:03:07,655
What I've got isn't
really a new invention,

75
00:03:07,655 --> 00:03:09,990
but it's a new kind
of guitar chord.

76
00:03:09,990 --> 00:03:13,093
What you do, it's for
the dynamic endings
of your rock show.

77
00:03:13,093 --> 00:03:15,830
This is the Mel Bay
chart right there.

78
00:03:15,830 --> 00:03:17,832
It's an A-minor augmented
with an Eddie Van Halen

79
00:03:17,832 --> 00:03:20,033
bolt action on the bottom.

80
00:03:20,033 --> 00:03:21,468
- Cool.

81
00:03:21,468 --> 00:03:23,037
- So when you get to the big
jams at the end of the concert,

82
00:03:23,037 --> 00:03:24,305
you can play it.

83
00:03:24,305 --> 00:03:26,773
Now, it takes two hands
to play this chord,

84
00:03:26,773 --> 00:03:29,677
so at that point you have
your dynamic front man,

85
00:03:29,677 --> 00:03:32,147
I guess Crow will do
it, will strum it.

86
00:03:32,147 --> 00:03:33,413
Okay, on the count of three.

87
00:03:33,413 --> 00:03:35,483
One, two, three.

88
00:03:35,483 --> 00:03:37,918
(guitar chord ringing)
(explosion bangs)

89
00:03:37,918 --> 00:03:40,488
(all coughing)

90
00:03:41,688 --> 00:03:43,390
What do you think, sirs?

91
00:03:43,390 --> 00:03:47,095
- Oh, great, get
Foghat on the phone.

92
00:03:47,095 --> 00:03:49,097
Well, our experiment
this week, Joel,

93
00:03:49,097 --> 00:03:51,565
is going to be sweeping
the country by storm.

94
00:03:51,565 --> 00:03:52,533
Isn't that right, Frank?

95
00:03:52,533 --> 00:03:54,035
That's right.

96
00:03:54,035 --> 00:03:55,270
You know all the fads with
the young people today.

97
00:03:55,270 --> 00:03:57,372
You know the kids today,
with their loud music,

98
00:03:57,372 --> 00:04:01,675
hula hoops, fax machines, but
the biggest fad these days,

99
00:04:01,675 --> 00:04:02,710
karaoke, woo!

100
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:08,717
What we've done is we've
invented a karaoke machine

101
00:04:08,717 --> 00:04:11,885
that exclusively plays
public domain songs.

102
00:04:11,885 --> 00:04:13,387
That's right, that
means you can sing

103
00:04:13,387 --> 00:04:15,957
into your karaoke machine,
have as much fun as you want,

104
00:04:15,957 --> 00:04:18,558
and not pay one cent
in artist royalties.

105
00:04:18,558 --> 00:04:20,093
- That's right, Frank.

106
00:04:20,093 --> 00:04:22,130
Now, what happens when you go
into your favorite karaoke bar

107
00:04:22,130 --> 00:04:24,098
and you want to hear "I
Want to Know What Love is"

108
00:04:24,098 --> 00:04:25,132
by Foreigner?

109
00:04:25,132 --> 00:04:26,667
- People vomit?

110
00:04:26,667 --> 00:04:30,505
- No, Lou Gramm, songwriter,
and Chess King, spokesmodel,

111
00:04:30,505 --> 00:04:34,275
gets a big fat royalty check,
and that means lots of money.

112
00:04:34,275 --> 00:04:36,277
So, Joel, we've
loaded our machine

113
00:04:36,277 --> 00:04:38,078
only with public domain songs.

114
00:04:38,078 --> 00:04:41,915
All free of copyright, all
owned by you, the people.

115
00:04:41,915 --> 00:04:43,150
- That's right.

116
00:04:43,150 --> 00:04:44,418
You wanna hit the
roll there, Jerry?

117
00:04:44,418 --> 00:04:46,120
- You get "The Battle
Hymn of the Republic."

118
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:47,755
- The immortal "Ba
Ba Black Sheep."

119
00:04:47,755 --> 00:04:50,958
- turgid and bittersweet
"Gregorian Chant number 5."

120
00:04:50,958 --> 00:04:53,127
- The impish "Twinkle
Twinkle Little Star."

121
00:04:53,127 --> 00:04:57,465
- Mozart's "The Magic Flute,"
and there's so much more.

122
00:04:57,465 --> 00:05:00,835
But your experiment this week,
Joel, is called "Pod People".

123
00:05:00,835 --> 00:05:02,470
It has nothing to do with pods.

124
00:05:02,470 --> 00:05:03,905
It has nothing to
do with people.

125
00:05:03,905 --> 00:05:07,608
It has everything
to do with hurting.

126
00:05:07,608 --> 00:05:09,110
And we're going to
sing you into it

127
00:05:09,110 --> 00:05:11,045
with our new public
domain karaoke machine.

128
00:05:11,045 --> 00:05:12,480
Hit it, Frank.

129
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:14,815
♪ Ave Maria ♪

130
00:05:21,388 --> 00:05:26,360
(all shouting)
(alarm blaring)

131
00:05:37,938 --> 00:05:41,575
- [Joel] Boy, it's a good thing
we got those new heads on.

132
00:05:41,575 --> 00:05:42,777
- [Tom] Great experiment, Joel.

133
00:05:42,777 --> 00:05:45,178
Great, great invention there.

134
00:05:46,313 --> 00:05:48,850
- [Joel] Oh, pod people
got no reason to live.

135
00:05:48,850 --> 00:05:50,785
- [Tom] Isn't that
Nia Peeples' brother?

136
00:05:50,785 --> 00:05:51,685
Pod Peeple?

137
00:05:52,620 --> 00:05:53,420
- [Joel] Mm-hmm.

138
00:05:53,420 --> 00:05:54,322
- [Tom] No.

139
00:05:55,957 --> 00:05:58,025
And she's married to
Maury Povich, right?

140
00:05:58,025 --> 00:06:00,193
- [Joel] No, you're
thinking of Connie Chung.

141
00:06:00,193 --> 00:06:02,130
That's Cheston.

142
00:06:02,130 --> 00:06:03,363
- [Tom] Sorry.

143
00:06:05,533 --> 00:06:06,900
- [Crow] Ooh.

144
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:08,202
- [Joel] William Anton.

145
00:06:08,202 --> 00:06:10,203
I think that's Susan
Anton's half-brother.

146
00:06:10,203 --> 00:06:11,038
- [Tom] Could be.

147
00:06:11,038 --> 00:06:11,872
- [Crow] Which half?

148
00:06:11,872 --> 00:06:12,707
- [Tom] Huh?

149
00:06:12,707 --> 00:06:15,008
- [Joel] Better half?

150
00:06:15,008 --> 00:06:16,943
Whoa, it's the Iron Maiden dude.

151
00:06:16,943 --> 00:06:18,112
Check him out.

152
00:06:19,113 --> 00:06:20,213
- [Crow] Hey!

153
00:06:21,282 --> 00:06:23,617
I believe I'll use my putter.

154
00:06:23,617 --> 00:06:25,252
Fore.

155
00:06:25,252 --> 00:06:26,487
- [Joel] For what?

156
00:06:26,487 --> 00:06:28,655
- [Crow] For hittin', guys.

157
00:06:33,393 --> 00:06:35,162
- [Joel] Oh, this is
where the Swamp Thing

158
00:06:35,162 --> 00:06:37,398
versus the sweet thang.

159
00:06:37,398 --> 00:06:38,232
- [Tom] Ooh.

160
00:06:38,232 --> 00:06:39,233
- [Crow] Ah.

161
00:06:41,202 --> 00:06:43,972
- [Tom] Ah, the
tears of a clown.

162
00:06:56,450 --> 00:06:58,318
- [Joel] Faruk Alatan?

163
00:06:58,318 --> 00:07:01,188
I went to school
with Faruk Alatan.

164
00:07:02,290 --> 00:07:03,557
(Tom chuckling)

165
00:07:03,557 --> 00:07:06,460
- [Tom] Let's go skin
it and eat it now.

166
00:07:08,762 --> 00:07:09,963
That's not a Spalding.

167
00:07:09,963 --> 00:07:12,133
It's a Piquer Simon-Jack
Gray signature ball.

168
00:07:12,133 --> 00:07:13,600
- [Crow] Huh.

169
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:16,137
I dare you to eat it.

170
00:07:16,137 --> 00:07:18,438
- [Tom] No, you eat it.

171
00:07:18,438 --> 00:07:19,607
Lick it first.

172
00:07:24,178 --> 00:07:27,013
- [Joel] That one's
hard on pants.

173
00:07:28,415 --> 00:07:31,552
Boy, that guy's got some
unfortunate skin, all right.

174
00:07:31,552 --> 00:07:32,787
- [Tom] Mm-hmm.

175
00:07:35,122 --> 00:07:36,857
- [Joel] Really
unfortunate skin.

176
00:07:36,857 --> 00:07:37,692
- [Tom] Yeah.

177
00:07:37,692 --> 00:07:39,593
- [Crow] Emergency 911.

178
00:07:40,828 --> 00:07:41,728
- [Tom] A man falls.

179
00:07:41,728 --> 00:07:43,197
Stunned by a monster.

180
00:07:43,197 --> 00:07:44,598
- [Joel] My baby.

181
00:07:45,667 --> 00:07:47,033
- [Tom] Oh, boldly backing away

182
00:07:47,033 --> 00:07:49,837
from where no man
has gone before.

183
00:07:50,905 --> 00:07:52,173
- [Crow] Car sick.

184
00:07:52,173 --> 00:07:54,675
(all humming)

185
00:07:55,843 --> 00:07:57,043
- [Tom] Look.

186
00:07:57,043 --> 00:07:58,345
Between a rock
and a hard planet.

187
00:07:58,345 --> 00:08:00,848
(Crow laughs)

188
00:08:02,283 --> 00:08:04,085
- [Tom] Come on and dance.

189
00:08:04,085 --> 00:08:06,453
- [Joel] I'm Curly
Neal, to show you how.

190
00:08:06,453 --> 00:08:08,355
- [Crow] It was a
dark and stormy night.

191
00:08:08,355 --> 00:08:10,858
I'd taken a creative
writing course.

192
00:08:10,858 --> 00:08:12,260
- [Joel] Daktari.

193
00:08:15,930 --> 00:08:20,267
- [Crow] Oh, I love
taking rides in the bog.

194
00:08:20,267 --> 00:08:21,368
- [Man] You sure
this is the place?

195
00:08:21,368 --> 00:08:22,903
- [Man] Sure I'm sure.

196
00:08:22,903 --> 00:08:24,872
- [Tom] I'm sure that you're
sure that I'm sure I'm sure.

197
00:08:24,872 --> 00:08:25,707
- [Crow] Sure?

198
00:08:25,707 --> 00:08:26,640
- [Tom] Sure.

199
00:08:26,640 --> 00:08:27,808
- [Man] Well, I think I'm sure.

200
00:08:27,808 --> 00:08:29,010
- [Tom] Think you're
sure I'm sure.

201
00:08:29,010 --> 00:08:30,277
- [Crow] Hey, does
he look dorky enough?

202
00:08:30,277 --> 00:08:31,545
(Tom laughing)

203
00:08:31,545 --> 00:08:33,147
- [Tom] Dorky Park.

204
00:08:34,315 --> 00:08:36,183
- And you think you
know it all, don't ya?

205
00:08:36,183 --> 00:08:37,718
- I just don't want
to spend a night out

206
00:08:37,718 --> 00:08:39,253
in this crap for nothin'.

207
00:08:39,253 --> 00:08:41,322
- For Christ's sake, will
you keep your voices down?

208
00:08:41,322 --> 00:08:42,757
There's rangers around here.

209
00:08:42,757 --> 00:08:44,492
- [Joel] Let's grab
the pic-a-nic baskets

210
00:08:44,492 --> 00:08:46,060
and scram, Boo-Boo.

211
00:08:48,295 --> 00:08:51,265
- [Tom] Meanwhile,
a few miles away.

212
00:08:54,702 --> 00:08:57,238
- [Joel] Darren, did
you just hear a comet?

213
00:08:57,238 --> 00:08:58,438
(thunder rumbling)

214
00:08:58,438 --> 00:08:59,707
- [Crow] Huh.

215
00:08:59,707 --> 00:09:00,507
Now, this guy's got
Renaissance Festival

216
00:09:00,507 --> 00:09:02,410
written all over him.

217
00:09:02,410 --> 00:09:04,145
- [Joel And Tom] Huzzah!

218
00:09:04,145 --> 00:09:07,080
(thunder rumbling)

219
00:09:09,417 --> 00:09:12,520
- [All] Lions and tigers,
and bears, oh, my!

220
00:09:12,520 --> 00:09:14,355
Lions and tigers, and bears.

221
00:09:14,355 --> 00:09:15,623
- [Joel] Oh, my!

222
00:09:15,623 --> 00:09:16,790
- [Tom] Well, time
to start camping.

223
00:09:16,790 --> 00:09:18,358
You dress up as
Oscar Wilde, and I'll

224
00:09:18,358 --> 00:09:20,227
